      "answer": "$\\mu=0.231$ (or $2\\sqrt3/15$).",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$\\frac12(35)v^2=250(1.05)$, so $v^2=15$, or use $-250=35a$ and $0=v^2+2(-50/7)(1.05)$, so $v^2=15$ — B1. Guidance: Correct work-energy equation on the horizontal section or use the $250$ N frictional force to set up an equation leading to the speed at the bottom.\n$R=35g\\cos30^\\circ=303.11$ — B1.\n$v^2=0+2a(2.5)=15$, leading to $a=3$, or PE change $=35g(2.5\\sin30^\\circ)=437.5$ — M1. Guidance: Use $v^2=u^2+2as$ with their $v^2$ to find $a$.\n$35g\\sin30^\\circ-F=35a$, or $175-F=35a$ — M1. Guidance: Newton's 2nd law down the slope, correct dimensions. Alternatively $35g(2.5\\sin30^\\circ)=F(2.5)+\\frac12(35)(15)$, or $437.5=2.5F+262.5$, using an energy equation with 3 relevant terms and correct dimensions.\n$F=\\mu R$ — M1. Guidance: $R$ must be a component of $35g$.\n$\\mu=0.231$ — A1. Guidance: Allow $\\mu=2\\sqrt3/15$ OE.\nAlternative method: $R=35g\\cos30^\\circ$ — B1; PE change $=35g(2.5\\sin30^\\circ)=437.5$ — B1; work done against friction on flat $=250(1.05)=262.5$ — B1; $35g(2.5\\sin30^\\circ)=F(2.5)+250(1.05)$ — M1, energy equation with 3 relevant terms and correct dimensions; $F=\\mu R$ — M1; $\\mu=0.231$ — A1, allow $2\\sqrt3/15$ OE.\nTotal: 6.",
